\boxed{\dfrac{846\sqrt{3}}{49}}

The first player can guarantee a maximum score of 6. 

By playing optimally (starting with the center and creating overlapping threats in the nine 3×3 squares), the first player can force at least one 3×3 square to contain six 1's. The second player has only 12 zeros and cannot simultaneously defend all nine squares to keep every one of them at five or fewer. A detailed strategy involves carefully selecting high-weight cells and responding to the second player’s moves to maintain pressure across multiple 3×3 regions, ensuring that at least one accumulates six 1's by the end of the game.

\boxed{6}

The probability distribution over positive integers \( i \) satisfies \( P(k) = \frac{3}{2} P(k+1) \) for all \( k \). This gives a geometric distribution: \( P(k) = p \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{k-1} \). Summing over all positive integers, \( p \sum_{k=1}^\infty \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{k-1} = p \cdot 3 = 1 \), so \( p = \frac{1}{3} \). Thus \( P(k) = \frac{1}{3} \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{k-1} \).

The decimal expansion of \( \frac{1}{7} = 0.\overline{142857} \) repeats every 6 digits. The digit 2 appears at positions \( i \equiv 3 \pmod{6} \), i.e., \( i = 3, 9, 15, \dots \) or \( i = 6m + 3 \) for \( m \ge 0 \).

The desired probability is the sum of probabilities for these positions:
\[
\sum_{m=0}^\infty P(6m+3) = \sum_{m=0}^\infty \frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{6m+2}
= \frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 \sum_{m=0}^\infty \left[\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^6\right]^m
= \frac{4}{27} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \frac{64}{729}}
= \frac{4}{27} \cdot \frac{729}{665}
= \frac{108}{665}.
\]

\boxed{\dfrac{108}{665}}

The condition means that \(109 = qx + 4\) for some integer \(q\), so \(109 - 4 = 105\) is a multiple of \(x\). Thus \(x\) must be a divisor of \(105\). The divisors of \(105 = 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 7\) are \(1, 3, 5, 7, 15, 21, 35, 105\). We require \(x\) to be a two-digit positive integer, so \(10 \le x \le 99\). The two-digit divisors are \(15, 21, 35\). Additionally, we need the remainder to be exactly \(4\), which requires \(x > 4\); all three satisfy this.

Sum = \(15 + 21 + 35 = 71\).

\boxed{71}

The endpoints of the line segment are \((-9, -2)\) and \((6, 8)\), both having integer coordinates. The difference in \(x\)-coordinates is \(6 - (-9) = 15\), and the difference in \(y\)-coordinates is \(8 - (-2) = 10\). The greatest common divisor of 15 and 10 is 5. The number of points on the segment with integer coordinates (including the endpoints) is \(\gcd(15, 10) + 1 = 5 + 1 = 6\).

We can verify by listing the points: \((-9, -2)\), \((-6, 0)\), \((-3, 2)\), \((0, 4)\), \((3, 6)\), \((6, 8)\). All coordinates are integers.

\boxed{6}

The maximum value is \(\frac{1}{12}\). 

Let \(f(t) = t^4 - t^5\) for \(t \in [0,1]\). We want to maximize \(\sum_{j=1}^n f(x_j)\) subject to \(\sum x_j = 1\) and \(x_j \ge 0\). For \(n=1\) the sum is \(0\), but for \(n \ge 2\) one can show via KKT conditions that any optimal configuration has at most two distinct positive values, and they must satisfy \(a+b=1\) and \(f'(a)=f'(b)\). Solving \(f'(t)=t^3(4-5t)\) gives the relation \(a(1-a)=1/6\), and the sum evaluates to \(ab(1-3ab)=1/12\). Adding zeros for \(n>2\) keeps the value unchanged, so \(\frac{1}{12}\) is the maximum for all \(n \ge 2\).

\boxed{\frac{1}{12}}

The cycle length containing a specific element (in this case, 1) in a uniformly random permutation of size \( N \) is uniformly distributed over \( \{1, 2, \dots, N\} \). Therefore, the expected value of this cycle length is \( \frac{N+1}{2} \).

With \( N = 2016 \), the expected value is \( \frac{2016+1}{2} = \frac{2017}{2} \).

\boxed{\frac{2017}{2}}

The problem asks for the number of ways to schedule a tournament of 9 distinct people over 4 ordered rounds, where each round partitions the 9 people into 3 unlabeled groups of 3, and every pair of people plays together exactly once. This is equivalent to counting the labeled resolvable Steiner triple systems of order 9 with ordered parallel classes (rounds). 

The unique Steiner triple system of order 9 is the affine plane of order 3, which has exactly 4 parallel classes. The number of labeled STS(9) is \(9! / |\operatorname{Aut}(STS(9))| = 362880 / 432 = 840\). Since the rounds are ordered, we multiply by the \(4! = 24\) ways to order the parallel classes, giving \(840 \times 24 = 20160\). Alternatively, one can build the schedule round by round: there are 280 choices for the first round, 36 for the second, 2 for the third, and 1 for the fourth, giving \(280 \times 36 \times 2 \times 1 = 20160\).

\boxed{20160}

The smallest possible value of \(n\) is \(17\).

To see why, note that the set of obtainable products modulo \(100\) must contain all \(100\) residue classes. We separate the products into units (coprime to \(100\)) and non-units (sharing a factor with \(100\)). The units can only be formed from cards that are themselves coprime to \(100\). For \(n\le 16\), the available units are \(\{1,3,7,9,11,13\}\); their subset products modulo \(100\) fail to generate all \(40\) units (for example, \(53\) is never produced). Thus \(n\ge 17\). When \(n=17\), the units become \(\{1,3,7,9,11,13,17\}\), whose subset products cover all \(40\) units. The non-unit cards up to \(17\) are \(\{2,4,5,6,8,10,12,14,15,16\}\). By forming a few base non-unit products (\(2,4,5,10,20=2\cdot10,50=5\cdot10,75=5\cdot15,0=2\cdot5\cdot10\)) and multiplying them by the already complete set of unit products, all \(60\) non-unit residues are obtained. No conflicts arise because the unit and non-unit cards are disjoint. Hence \(n=17\) is minimal.

\boxed{17}

